  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 230 PM CST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nSatellite imagery shows a large are of clearing this morning across \nthe central part of the country. A Canadian surface high pressure \nmoving in from the northwest continues to advect cooler air \nsoutheastward keeping temperatures in the low to mid 40s across the \nregion. The good news is that this surface high pressure system has \npushed the compressed pressure gradient eastward which has taken \nwith it the gusty conditions we have seen over the past 2 to 3 days. \nWinds through the afternoon continue from the north northwest around \n5-10 MPH. There could be some gusts to 15 MPH just adding some chill \nto the air. Clouds are beginning to encroach into the region from \nthe north and higher level clouds to the west. These should envelope \nthe region overnight. This will likely slow down some effects of \nradiational cooling overnight although overnight lows are still \nexpected to remain in the lower to middle 20s.\n\nContinued northerly flow at the low to mid levels keeps temperatures \nin the upper 30s to mid 40s through the week. As the Thanksgiving \nholiday approaches, a large scale trough digs to the SE CONUS. This \nsystem is expected to form into a fairly stout east coastal storm. \nThere will be two factors that make this system relevant to our \ninterests, the first is that the spatial scale of the circulation \nstretches from the North Carolina coast all the way to the Wabash \nand Mississippi Valley which may facilitate some wrap around \nprecipitation extending into eastern MO Thursday morning and \ncontinuing through Friday. Second is the upper level make up of this \nsynoptic scale system. While low to mid level analysis shows an \nexpansive low, the 500mb profile depicts two distinct systems within \nthe larger trough acting as more of a binary system as the end of \nthe week arrives. This secondary system aloft add additional lift \nacross the Mississippi Valley which could facilitate more \nopportunities for precipitation. Diurnal temperatures swing across \nthe freezing line through the week meaning that this could be our \nfirst opportunity to see some wintry precipitation in the region \nThursday morning. The extent of this precipitation will be \ndetermined by the path and expanse of the storm system which models \nhave been wavering a bit. The latest runs has shifted slightly west \nwith the National Blend projecting a 5-15 percent chance for a \ndusting or greater of snow along and east of US Highway 65 \nincreasing to 30-50 percent chances along and east of US Highway 63. \nBumping that threshold up to an inch of snow decreases probabilities \ndramatically only topping out around 25 percent along the US-63 \ncorridor and little to none west of there. There is still a lot of \nuncertainty as long range ensemble guidance does tend to broad brush \nprecipitation potential in the extended. Deterministic guidance \ntrends show a distinct cut off in potential snow accumulations with \ntracks across all deterministic guidance varying from west to east \nwith more favoring a bit easterly. Where confidence is higher is \nthat temperatures are expected to rise above freezing (60-80 percent \nchances) Thursday so any snow accumulations are expected to melt \nnotably to completely through the afternoon.\n\nLooking toward the tail end of the holiday weekend into early next \nweek, upper level flow becomes more zonal resulting in the overall \npattern becoming more progressive. Guidance progs another wave \nmigrating across the central CONUS which could bring an opportunity \nfor precipitation Monday into Tuesday mainly across far northern MO. \nTemperatures during the overnight period do look to be right around \nthe freezing mark which will affect precipitation type expectations; \nhowever, uncertainty remains very high with this portion of the \nforecast and I expect several changes between today and next\nweek.",
